# ENUMERATION CHECK
The task works with documents. A step may need to enumerate ALL items of a source (scenes, chapters, sections, list entries, requirements, characters, dates, amounts, etc.), and later steps act on each item.
- An "llm" step sees only previous step results and does NOT read the document. The plan is invalid when an "llm" step is asked to enumerate, split or extract ALL items of a document and that step itself does not read the document. A preceding "rlm"/"rag" step counts only when its goal and criterion already enumerated THE SAME items (or the whole source). If the previous step described only characters/places while the "llm" step must enumerate scenes/sections, that is not a pass.
- "rlm" reads the entire attached document. "rag" returns fragments; enumerating from it is valid only when the "rag" step comes before the "llm" step and its goal is to find exactly the enumerated items.
- The enumeration step's goal and checkpoint_criteria must demand ALL items, numbered or unambiguously identified, with no reduction to "key" items.
- Every item that needs an action must have its own step with the item's description verbatim and "source_step" pointing to the enumeration step. When the item count is not known yet, that is allowed — the reviewer adds the steps.
- An "llm" step that composes an answer from an already obtained "rlm"/"rag" result is valid.
Do not reject the plan for other reasons: style, step count, or tool choice outside enumeration.

